      "side": 1,
      "message": "On Zephyr, `fp_configure_detect()` at L366 enables the sensor GPIO IRQ (`FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_DETECT`), whereas on legacy EC `GPIO_FPS_INT` is only enabled when `mode \u0026 FP_MODE_ANY_WAIT_IRQ`.\n\nBecause `FP_MODE_FINGER_UP` is polled (`!(mode \u0026 FP_MODE_ANY_WAIT_IRQ)`) and the user\u0027s finger is still resting on the sensor after capture/match, leaving the GPIO IRQ enabled on Zephyr triggers an immediate `TASK_EVENT_SENSOR_IRQ` wakeup (where the driver\u0027s GPIO ISR `*_irq()` then disables the IRQ).\n\nWhen `!(mode \u0026 FP_MODE_ANY_WAIT_IRQ)`, we should call `fp_idle()` on Zephyr to disable the GPIO IRQ (since `FINGERPRINT_SENSOR_MODE_IDLE` in all 6 Zephyr drivers only calls `*_disable_irq(dev)` without exiting detect mode on the sensor):\n```cpp\nif (mode \u0026 FP_MODE_ANY_DETECT_FINGER) {\n#ifdef CONFIG_ZEPHYR\n\tif (!(mode \u0026 FP_MODE_ANY_WAIT_IRQ))\n\t\tfp_idle();\n#else\n\tif (mode \u0026 FP_MODE_ANY_WAIT_IRQ) {\n\t\tgpio_clear_pending_interrupt(GPIO_FPS_INT);\n\t\tgpio_enable_interrupt(GPIO_FPS_INT);\n\t}\n#endif\n}\n```",
      "revId": "8ca06a6cfb1c299d696109d11a2bc1c0a0ce104d",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
